{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLpTzU7K7phuDPn94eXJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`(1/Sum(1, (p, 8, 6))).as_real_imag()` now returns `(nan, nan)`, matching how `Pow(0, -1)` behaves. I ran `sympy/core`, `sympy/functions/elementary` and `sympy/concrete`. All 2470 tests passed, with no failures.\n\nThe fix is in `Pow.as_real_imag` in `sympy/core/power.py`. In the integer-exponent branch where the base has no imaginary part, a negative exponent now returns `(nan, nan)` when two things hold:\n- The base isn't known to be nonzero.\n- The base has no free symbols.\n\nI limited it to bases without free symbols on purpose. A real symbol `x` still gives `(1/x, 0)` for `x**-1`, so existing results for symbolic real bases don't change.\n\nI first tried returning `(re(self), im(self))`, but that recurses infinitely, so I went with `(nan, nan)`.\n\nA constant base that can't be proven nonzero now gives `(nan, nan)`. A symbolic base that could be zero, such as a real `x`, still gives `(1/x, 0)`, so the original inconsistency remains for symbols. I couldn't find a way to cover them without breaking the real-symbol results.\n\nI didn't add a test, since test files are off limits."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":944,"cache_read_input_tokens":25593,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":944},"output_tokens":436,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f53a14e5-085a-45af-8acf-5368c48d1dd9","uuid":"bbc4ddda-77b1-4aa2-a966-26451fde0fe9","timestamp":"2026-11-24T00:38:08.620Z","request_id":"req_011ChLpTzDj4YYKp4mAM5C7Q"}
